WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:04.090
Where I come from

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:05.450
societies are quite divided.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:07.129
There are visible walls

00:00:07.129 --> 00:00:09.610
protecting against supposed dangers,

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:11.730
walls marking limits of properties, marking
borders,

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:14.360
or separating ethnic or religious groups.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.350
But there are also invisible walls:

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.369
the ones in the minds of people.

00:00:18.369 --> 00:00:20.699
I grew up in Peru, Mexico, Bolivia,

00:00:20.699 --> 00:00:22.070
Germany and the former-Yugoslavia.

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:24.199
And if nothing else they all had

00:00:24.199 --> 00:00:25.590
that one thing in common.

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.609
I have always wondered what kind of stories

00:00:27.609 --> 00:00:29.300
I can find on both sides of the wall.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.180
This is why I became a journalist.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.660
Walls are borders based on fears and clichés.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:36.923
But as it turns out, the stories on both sides

00:00:36.923 --> 00:00:39.730
are often very similar.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:41.170
With fake news running rampant,

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:44.640
it’s more important than ever to go out
and talk to people.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.060
To understand their point of view.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.280
To keep on being interested and curious

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.200
on both sides of the wall,

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.300
because the truth is never going to be only
on one side.

